From 442717444eb63e6912e30ca2424ef01812ef0445 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ahsan Hussain <ahsan.hussain@siemens.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 18:27:45 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/8] vrend: sample external-only dma-bufs through a shadow
 texture

EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import_modifiers lets a driver report a
format/modifier pair as external-only. Such images can only be bound to
GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES and sampled through samplerExternalOES; binding
them to GL_TEXTURE_2D is not supported. vrend always binds imported
dma-bufs to a regular texture target (gl_id), and the guest's shaders
sample it as sampler2D, so these imports either fail or, worse, sample
undefined data without any GL error.

NVIDIA's proprietary driver (tested with 595.58.03 on aarch64) reports
DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR as external-only for DRM_FORMAT_ARGB8888 and
DRM_FORMAT_XRGB8888. LINEAR is what a guest compositor on virgl gets from
a Venus client whenever the client's WSI uses its prime-blit path: e.g.
when the host does not provide tiled Venus interop buffers, or with
compositors such as KWin and wlroots that add LINEAR to their dma-buf
feedback. On this driver glEGLImageTargetTexture2DOES(GL_TEXTURE_2D) of
such an image is accepted without error, but GL_TEXTURE_2D sampling
returns scrambled data, while the same image sampled as
samplerExternalOES is correct. The only workaround so far is
MESA_VK_WSI_DEBUG=sw in the guest, which costs a CPU copy per frame.

Handle such resources with a shadow texture:
 - In vrend_renderer_pipe_resource_set_type(), query
   eglQueryDmaBufModifiersEXT() (new helper
   virgl_egl_dmabuf_is_external_only()) for the imported
   format/modifier. If the pair is external-only, import the EGL image
   into a separate GL_TEXTURE_EXTERNAL_OES texture (egl_external_id) and
   give gl_id ordinary storage of the same size and format. gl_id is then
   no longer an EGL image, so VREND_STORAGE_EGL_IMAGE is cleared for it
   and the copy/blit paths no longer treat the resource as one.
 - Before the resource is read (sampler views bound for a draw, source of
   resource_copy_region and blit, and transfers from host), copy the
   external texture into gl_id with a small fullscreen draw in the
   blitter context (vrend_blitter_refresh_external()), followed by
   glFlush() and vrend_sync_make_current() so that the guest context
   sees the result.
 - The dma-buf is produced by another device or API, and the guest has to
   synchronize with that producer (e.g. implicit fences or a
   wait-for-buffer in the compositor) before it submits the commands that
   read the resource. The shadow therefore only has to be refreshed once
   per command submission: vrend_decode_ctx_submit_cmd() bumps a global
   submission counter and each resource remembers the counter value of
   its last refresh. Transfers happen outside of a command stream and
   always refresh.
 - Desktop GL has no extension that exposes samplerExternalOES to GLSL
   1.30+, yet NVIDIA accepts "#version 330" with
   GL_OES_EGL_image_external and texture2D(samplerExternalOES, ...) while
   not advertising the extension in its GL extension string. So on
   desktop GL, support is determined by compiling the copy program once.
   On GLES, GL_OES_EGL_image_external_essl3 is required and the program
   uses GLSL ES 3.00; the BGRA channel swap that vrend applies to BGRA
   formats on GLES hosts is folded into the copy shader.
 - If the copy program is not available, a warning is logged and the
   resource is imported as before.

Limitations:
 - Only single-level, single-sample GL_TEXTURE_2D resources are handled;
   other external-only imports fail with EINVAL instead of silently
   sampling garbage.
 - The shadow is a read-only mirror. Guest GL rendering into such a
   resource lands in gl_id only and is overwritten by the next refresh.
   For the case that motivated this (client buffers sampled by a
   compositor) the guest never renders into the imported buffer.

Drivers that do not report the imported pair as external-only are
unaffected: no extra texture, no copies.

Tested on an NVIDIA host (595.58.03) with a Venus + virgl guest running
Weston, the client buffers imported as LINEAR, without MESA_VK_WSI_DEBUG:
 - vkcube and vkmark --winsys=wayland render correctly instead of
   scrambled.
 - vkmark --winsys=wayland: 2431, vs. 1730 with MESA_VK_WSI_DEBUG=sw and
   2360-2420 for the same LINEAR import without the shadow (which renders
   garbage), i.e. the shadow copy costs no measurable throughput here.
   Weston composites, and therefore refreshes, about 200 times per
   second; each refresh of the 800x600 window buffer takes about 105 us
   on the vrend thread.
 - vkmark --winsys=headless: 8568, vs. about 2500 with
   MESA_VK_WSI_DEBUG=sw.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Closes: https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/virgl/virglrenderer/-/issues/651
Signed-off-by: Ahsan Hussain <ahsan.hussain@siemens.com>
